  "text": "Participants with EPTB were almost four times more likely to achieve a successful treatment outcome than participants with pulmonary TB (AOR = 3.8; 95% CI = 1.23–11.7; p = 0.023). This finding contrasts with most previous studies [7, 9, 20, 21, 23, 27], and several factors may account for the difference. First, some forms of EPTB, such as lymph node involvement, are more localized and less aggressive than pulmonary disease. Second, when the infection remains confined to specific sites, patients may mount a stronger immune response. Third, patients with pulmonary TB face additional challenges, including respiratory complications such as respiratory failure, which can complicate treatment and lead to poorer outcomes. Previous studies have reported variable treatment success rates for EPTB, depending on the site of infection, HIV co-morbidity, and age [37, 38]: patients with pleural, lymph node or abdominal involvement had better outcomes, whereas those with central nervous system involvement had poorer outcomes [37]. Because our data could not distinguish among specific types of EPTB, future studies should collect and report EPTB type so that these differences can be examined more directly."
